From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jake Cattrall <krazyjakee@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 03:09:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Repaint animation frames by what they change

While any CSS animation runs, every frame restyled, repainted and
re-rendered the whole window: a 14px spinner cost 4.7ms of CPU a frame.
Now a frame whose only cause is an animation tick costs what it changes:

- `BaseDocument::paint_damage()` says what such a frame changed: nothing,
  a rectangle (where the animating elements painted last frame and where
  they paint now), or everything (layout damage, or anything it can't
  bound). The painter records, for each animating element, the transform
  and clip its parent painted it with; nothing above it moves in such a
  frame, so its new bounds follow from that and its new transform.
- `paint_scene_damaged` culls to the damage, and layers whose clip covers
  the damage clip to it instead (same pixels, far less work for renderers
  whose cost follows a layer's area). Box-path containment closes the
  open paths Blitz builds.
- The shell repaints only the damage when the renderer supports it
  (`WindowRenderer::render_damage`), keeps the last frame when nothing
  visible changed, and wakes on a timer rather than spinning when it
  didn't present. Any other cause of a frame still repaints everything.
  `BLITZ_DAMAGE=0` turns this off, for comparisons.
- Animation sets with nothing to tick (paused, finished) are no longer
  restyled every frame.
- When every running animation changes only in steps (`steps()` timing),
  `next_animation_change()` says when the next step is due and the shell
  sleeps until then: a `steps(48)` loop over 40s ticks 1.2 times a second,
  not 60.

Measured on an RTX 3090 / Ryzen 9 5900X at 60 frames a second: a running
spinner went from 44% of a core to 8% (from 4.7 to ~1.4ms a frame), an
8-step spinner to 1.7%; idle stays at 0.25%.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
